          %h1#frchet_spaces Fréchet spaces
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#nonexample"} Non-Example
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#duals"} Duals
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#AsProjectiveLimits"} As projective limits
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#path_smoothness"} Path smoothness
              %li
                %a{:href => "#DifferentiableAndSmoothFunctions"} Differentiable and smooth functions
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Fréchet spaces are particularly well-behaved
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+spaces"} topological vector spaces
            (TVSes). Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            is a Fréchet space, but Fréchet spaces may have non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
            on Fréchet spaces, yet they are more general than
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+spaces"} Banach spaces
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite-dimensional+manifolds"} infinite-dimensional manifolds
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifolds"} Fréchet manifolds
          %p
            A basic example of a Fréchet space is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo ≔
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mo ⟵
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\infty \coloneqq \underset{\longleftarrow}{\lim} \mathbb{R}^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+limit"} projective limit
            over the finite dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ProjRInfinity"}
            below) . This is not a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
            anymore, since it does not carry a compatible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norm"} norm
            anymore (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Saunders89"} Saunders 89
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn ∞
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi p
                        %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi n
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\infty \overset{p^n}{\longrightarrow} \mathbb{R}^n \overset{\Vert -\Vert_n}{\longrightarrow} \mathbb{R}
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^n
            is the defining
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            and where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\Vert -\Vert}_n
            is the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norm"} norm
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/seminorms"} seminorms
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\infty
          %p
            Generally, a Fréchet space is equivalently a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+space"} real vector space
            equipped with a countable familiy of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/seminorms"} seminorms
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FrechetSpaceAsCompleteTVSWithCompatibleSeminorms"}
            below.
          %p
            Beware the clash of terminology: a ‘Fréchet topology’ or a ‘Fréchet topological space’ is something different; this just means that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            satisfies the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axiom"} separation axiom
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+spaces"} topological vector spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            and
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/properties"} properties
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            There are two inequivalent definitions of Fréchet spaces found in the literature. The original definition due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Banach"} Stefan Banach
            defines Fréchet spaces as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metrizable+topological+space"} metrizable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+space"} complete
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+spaces"} topological vector spaces
          %p
            In 1953
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bourbaki"} Bourbaki
            (Topological vector spaces, Section II.4.1) added the condition of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+convex+vector+space"} local convexity
          %p
            The term “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-space"}> F-space
            ” can refer to either of these definitions, although in the modern literature it is more commonly used to refer to the non-locally convex notion. The books of Köthe (1969) and Schaefer (1971) require F-spaces to be locally convex.
          %p There are various equivalent definitions of locally convex Fréchet spaces:
          #FrechetSpaceAsMetisableCompleteTVS.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong Fréchet space
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+space"} complete
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+convex+vector+space"} locally convex vector space
              that is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metrisable+topological+space"} metrisable
          #FrechetSpaceAsCompleteTVSWithCompatibleSeminorms.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              %strong
                (via systems of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/seminorms"} seminorms
            %p
              A
              %strong Fréchet space
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+space"} complete
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topology
              may be given (as a
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+space"} gauge space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
              family of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/seminormed+vector+space"} seminorms
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/seminorms"} seminorms
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    {\Vert - \Vert}_n \;\colon\; V \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
                    \\,,
                    \\;\;\;
                    n \in \mathbb{N}
            %p
              such that the set of all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+balls"} open balls
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi B
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi V
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                          %mi x
                          %mo −
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi n
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo }
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi for
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi V
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    B_\epsilon^{(n)}(x)
                    \\coloneqq
                    \\left\{
                    y \in V \,\vert\,  {\Vert x-y \Vert}_n \lt \epsilon
                    \\right\}
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    for
                    \\;
                    x \in V\,, \epsilon \gt 0 \,, n \in \mathbb{N}
            %p
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+of+neighborhoods"} base of neighborhoods
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              We accept as an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
              of Fréchet spaces any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/seminorms"} seminorms
              used to prove that a space is a Fréchet space is
              %em not
              required to be preserved. More generally, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              of Fréchet spaces are the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous
              linear maps, so that Fréchet spaces form a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              of the category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi TVS
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TVS
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+spaces"} topological vector spaces
          %h2#examples Examples
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
              is a Fréchet space.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+map"} smooth maps
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a Fréchet space. This can be extended to some non-compact manifolds, in particular when
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
          #SchwartSpaceOfFunctionsIsFrechetSpace.num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwartz+space"} Schwartz space
                is a Fréchet space)
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwartz+space"} Schwartz space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
              of functions with rapidly decreasing derivatives (
              %a{:href => "Schwartz+space#SchwartzSpaceOfFunctionsWithRapidlyDecreasingDerivatives"}> this def.
              ) is a Fréchet space.
          %p
            See
            %a{:href => "Schwartz+space#SchwartSpaceOfFunctionsIsFrechetSpace"} this prop.
          .num_example
            %h6#nonexample Non-Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+space"} Lebesgue space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^p(\mathbb{R})
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \lt 1
              is
              %em not
              a Fréchet space, because it is not locally convex.
          #ProjRInfinity.num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              Consider the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+product"} direct product
              (as
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+spaces"} topological vector spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %p
              Equivalently the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+limit"} projective limit
              (as
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+spaces"} topological vector spaces
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %munder
                        %mi lim
                        %mo ⟵
                      %mi n
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mo ⟵
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 2
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 0
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{R}^\infty
                    \\coloneqq
                    \\underset{\longleftarrow}{\lim}_n \mathbb{R}^n
                    \=
                    \\underset{\longleftarrow}{\lim}
                    \\left(
                    \\cdots \to \mathbb{R}^2 \overset{}{\to} \mathbb{R}^1 \to \mathbb{R}^0
                    \\right)
            %p
              over all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
              via their canonical
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+maps"} projection maps
            %p
              (Beware that the same symbol “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\infty
              ” is also used for the limit of the same sequence but with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              with discrete topology, what leads to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linearly+compact+vector+space"} linearly compact vector space
              as well as for the
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+limit"} inductive limit
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ↪
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 3
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi …
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}\to \mathbb{R}^2\hookrightarrow\mathbb{R}^3\hookrightarrow\ldots
            %p Write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_n \colon \mathbb{R}^\infty \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^n
            %p
              for the induced
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
              maps onto the first
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              copies and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|\cdot\|_n
              be the canonical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norm"} norm
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %p
              Then a compatible countable family of seminorms on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\infty
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FrechetSpaceAsCompleteTVSWithCompatibleSeminorms"}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo ↦
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi v
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                          %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \mapsto {\Vert\pi_n(v) \Vert_n}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\infty
              becomes a Fréchet space.
          %p
            On the other hand, the locally convex direct sum of a countable number of copies of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is not a Fréchet space.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#general General
          %p
            Fréchet spaces are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/barrelled"} barrelled
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bornological"} bornological
          %h3#duals Duals
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+space"} dual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
            of a Fréchet space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is itself again a Fréchet space precisely only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is in fact a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
          %p
            This follows from the statement paragraph 29.1 (7) in (
            %a{:href => "#Koethe"}> Koethe
            ), which is: The strong dual of a locally convex metrizable TVS
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is metrizable iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is normable.
          %p
            See also (
            %a{:href => "#Saunders89"}> Saunders 89, p. 255
            ).
          %h3#AsProjectiveLimits As projective limits
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+topological+space"} complete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+convex+topological+vector+space"} locally convex topological vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofiltered+category"} cofiltered
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+limit"} projective limit
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+spaces"} Banach spaces
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+convex+spaces"} locally convex spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %em metrisable
          %p
            To see this, choose a base
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi α
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_{\alpha}\}_{\alpha \in A}
            of the neighborhood filter of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_{\alpha}
            be Minkowski functional associated to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{\alpha}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{\alpha}
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, p_{\alpha})
            is easily seen to be a Banach space and because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is directed by reverse inclusion so is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{\alpha}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mo ⟵
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \underset{\longleftarrow}{\lim} X_{\alpha}
            in the category of locally convex spaces. For details, see (
            %a{:href => "#SchaeferWolff99"}> Schaefer-Wolff 99, Chapter II.5
            ,
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://books.google.com/books?id=9kXY742pABoC&pg=PA51"} page 51ff
          %p
            Now given that a Fréchet space admits a
            %em decreasing sequence
            of convex balanced and absorbing neighborhoods, it follows immediately that:
          %p
            Every Fréchet space is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+limit"} sequential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+limit"} projective limit
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+spaces"} Banach spaces
          %p Conversely, any limit of a countable sequence of Banach spaces is a Fréchet space.
          %p
            See (
            %a{:href => "#SchaeferWolff99"}> Schaefer-Wolff 99
            ), Chapter II.4, page 48f (as well as Theorem I.6.1, page 28).
          %p
            (from
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "http://math.stackexchange.com/a/53020/58526"} this math.stackexchange comment
          %p
            See also example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ProjRInfinity"}
            below.
          %h3#path_smoothness Path smoothness
          #PathSmoothFunction.num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              %strong (path smooth linear function)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              be a Fréchet vector space (def.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FrechetSpaceAsCompleteTVSWithCompatibleSeminorms"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+function"} linear function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mu \;\colon\; V \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
            %p
              is called
              %em path smooth
              if for every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    g \;\colon\; \mathbb{R} \longrightarrow V
            %p
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composite
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi g
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mu \circ g \;\colon\; \mathbb{R} \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
            %p
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
          #PathSmoothLinearFunctionsOnFrechetSpaceAreContinuous.num_defn
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong (path-smooth linear functions on a Fréchet space are continuous)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              be a Fréchet vector space (def.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FrechetSpaceAsCompleteTVSWithCompatibleSeminorms"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+function"} linear function
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              which is path-smooth (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PathSmoothFunction"}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#MoerdijkReyes91"}> Moerdijk-Reyes 91, chapter II, lemma 3.7
            )
          %p
            Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PathSmoothLinearFunctionsOnFrechetSpaceAreContinuous"}
            implies for instance that
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions+are+the+smooth+linear+functionals"} distributions are the smooth linear functionals
          %h2#DifferentiableAndSmoothFunctions Differentiable and smooth functions
          %p
            It is possible to generalize some aspects of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
            (differential calculus) to Fréchet spaces (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Michor80"} Michor 80, chapter 8
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Saunders89"} Saunders 89, p. 256
          %p
            For example the definition of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative"} derivative
            of a curve is simply the same as in finite dimensions:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              For a continuous path in a Fréchet space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(t)
              we define
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi h
                        %mo →
                        %mn 0
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo +
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f'(t) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{1}{h} (f(t + h) - f(t))
            %p
              If the limit exists and is continuous, we say that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is continuously differentiably or
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^1
          %p
            And just as in the finite dimensional case, we can define the partial derivative, or rather: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directional+derivative"} directional
            or Gâteaux derivative:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_7 Definition
            %p
              %strong directional derivative
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              be Fréchet spaces,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq F
              open and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo :
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P: U \to G
              a nonlinear continuous map. The derivative of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              at the point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in U
              in the direction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \in F
              is the map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mi P
                    %mo :
                    %mi U
                    %mo ×
                    %mi F
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    D P: U \times F \to G
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi h
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo →
                        %mn 0
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                    %mi t
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    D P(f) h = \lim_{t \to 0} \frac{1}{t} ( P(f + t h) - P(f))
            %p
              If the limit exists and is jointly continuous in both variables we say that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              is continuous differentiable or
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^1
          %p A simple, but nontrivial example is the operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  P: C^{\infty}[a, b] \to C^{\infty}[a, b]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi f
                  %mi f
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  P(f) \coloneqq f f'
          %p with the derivative
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi h
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo ′
                  %mi h
                  %mo +
                  %mi f
                  %mi h
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  D P(f) h = f'h + f h'
          %p
            It is possible to generalize the Riemann integral to Fréchet spaces, too: For a continuous path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(t)
            on an interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a, b]
            in a Fréchet space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            we look for an element
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi d
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_a^b f(t) d t \in F
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              There exists a unique element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi d
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_a^b f(t) d t \in F
              such that
            %p
              (i) for every continuous functional
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              we have
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msubsup
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi d
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msubsup
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi d
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(\int_a^b f(t) d t) = \int_a^b \phi(f(t)) d t
            %p
              (ii) for every continuous seminorm
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\| \cdot \|}
              we have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %msubsup
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi d
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo ≤
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi d
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\| \int_a^b f(t) d t \|} \leq \int_a^b {\| f(t) \|} d t
            %p (iii) integration is linear and
            %p
              (iv) additive, i.e.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi d
                    %mi t
                    %mo +
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi b
                      %mi c
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi d
                    %mi t
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi a
                      %mi c
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi d
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_a^b f(t) d t + \int_b^c f(t) d t = \int_a^c f(t) d t
          %p There is a version of the fundamental theorem of calculus:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              If P is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                    %mi t
                    %mi h
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Domain
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f + t h \in Domain(P)
              for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi t
                      %mo ≤
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq t \leq 1
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mn 0
                      %mn 1
                    %mi D
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                    %mi t
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi h
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi d
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    P(f + h) - P(f) = \int_0^1 D P(f + t h) \;h \; d t
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+rule"} chain rule
            is valid:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              are
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^1
              then so is their composition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q \circ P
              and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi h
                    %mo =
                    %mi D
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi D
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    D [Q \circ P](f) h = D Q(P(f)) \; D P(f) \; h
          %p
            The first derivative
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D P
            is a function of two variables, the base point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            and the direction
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D P
            is already linear in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            only:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_8 Definition
            %p
              %strong second derivative
              The second derivative of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              in the direction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              is defined to be
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mn 2
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi h
                    %mo ,
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo →
                        %mn 0
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                    %mi t
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi h
                    %mo −
                    %mi D
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    D^2 P(f) (h, k) = \lim_{t \to 0} \frac{1}{t} (D P(f + t k) h - D P(f) h)
          %p
            It is a theorem that the second derivative, if it exists and is jointly continuous, is bilinear in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi h
                    %mo ,
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (h ,k)
          %p
            We can iterate this procedure to define derivatives of arbitrary order, and thus the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %strong smooth functions between Fréchet spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifolds"} Fréchet manifolds
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#Vogt00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dietmar+Vogt"}> Dietmar Vogt
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lectures on Fréchet spaces
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www2.math.uni-wuppertal.de/~vogt/vorlesungen/fs.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                PlanetMath,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://planetmath.org/frechetspace"} Fréchet space
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://secure.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/wiki/Fréchet_space"} Fréchet space
            %li#Koethe
              %p
                Gottfried Koethe:
                %em Topological Vector Spaces I
            %li#SchaeferWolff99
              %p
                H. Schaefer, Manfred Wolff
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "http://books.google.com/books?id=9kXY742pABoC"} Topological Vector Spaces
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Kriegl"}> Andreas Kriegl
                ,
                %em Fréchet Spaces
                lecture notes, 2016 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~kriegl/Skripten/2016SS.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p Discussion of analysis on Fréchet spaces includes
          %ul
            %li#Michor80
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Michor"}> Peter Michor
                , chapter 8 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Manifolds of differentiable mappings
                %a{:href => "http://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~michor/manifolds_of_differentiable_mappings.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Richard S. Hamilton:
                %em The Inverse Function Theorem of Nash and Moser
                (Bulletin (New Series) of the American Mathematical Society Volume 7, Number 1, July 1982)
          %p
            Discussion in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundles"} jet bundles
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+pro-manifolds"} locally pro-manifolds
            includes
          %ul
            %li#Saunders89
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Saunders"}> David Saunders
                , chapter 7 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The geometry of jet bundles
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 142
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Khavkine"}> Igor Khavkine
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Synthetic+variational+calculus"} Synthetic geometry of differential equations: I. Jets and comonad structure
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.06238"}> arXiv:1701.06238
                )
          %p
            Discussion in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            includes
          %ul
            %li#MoerdijkReyes91
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"} Gonzalo Reyes
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Models+for+Smooth+Infinitesimal+Analysis"} Models for Smooth Infinitesimal Analysis
          %p
            Refinement to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            by suitable smoothed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star-algebras"} C-star-algebras
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              Nikolay Ivankov,
              %em Unbounded bivariant K-theory and an Approach to Noncommutative Fréchet spaces
              %a{:href => "http://hss.ulb.uni-bonn.de/2011/2624/2624.pdf"} pdf
